    "answer": "Now I have a thorough understanding of the codebase. Here's my analysis and solution options.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Root cause**: The stage board timer lives in `StageRowViewModel` \u2014 it starts ticking when `MarkRunning()` is called (triggered by a `stage_start` event), and stops only when `Status` leaves `\"Running\"` (triggered by `stage_done`/`stage_report`/`flagged` events). However, the method that applies events to the stage board (`ApplyStageEventToBoard` in `MainWindowViewModel.Helpers.cs`) is called **only for the selected task** \u2014 there's an early return guard at line ~69: `if (relayEvent.TaskId != SelectedTask?.Id) return;`. A `flagged` event for a non-selected task therefore never updates the stage card's status, so it remains `\"Running\"` and the 1-second `_elapsedTimer` keeps calling `RefreshElapsed()` on it perpetually.\n\n**Scenario**: Task A runs, the review stage starts (card shows `\"Running 2m 03s\"`). Task A gets flagged. If Task A is the selected task, the `flagged` event hits `ApplyStageEventToBoard` and sets status to `\"Flagged\"` \u2192 timer stops. But if another task is selected (or the user switched away), the `flagged` event is silently skipped for the stage board, and the card continues ticking indefinitely. This is especially visible when a drain starts the next task \u2014 the previous task's review stage card appears stuck on \"Running\".\n\n---\n\n## Solution Options\n\n### Option A: Process `flagged` events for the stage board unconditionally\n\nIn `HandleRelayEvent` (in `MainWindowViewModel.Helpers.cs`), extract the stage-board update for `flagged` events to **before** the selected-task guard, so it fires regardless of which task is selected. The `flagged` event always means \"this stage stopped\" \u2014 it should update the board's status so the timer stops, even if the user is looking at a different task.\n\n*Minimal, targeted change \u2014 one additional condition block in `HandleRelayEvent`.*\n\n### Option B: Clean up stale \"Running\" stage states when a new task begins\n\nIn `ResetStages` (in `MainWindowViewModel.Stages.cs`) and/or in `BeginRunningTask`/`OnExecuteStarted`, explicitly stop any stage that is still `\"Running\"` when a new task starts. Since stages are per-task in concept but live in a shared collection, starting a new task should first settle any orphaned running timer.\n\n*More defensive \u2014 catches all edge cases (flagged without event, crash, etc.) but slightly heavier.*\n\n### Option C: Move the board update into `UpdateRunningTask`\n\n`UpdateRunningTask` (in `MainWindowViewModel.Helpers.cs`) already processes `flagged` events for any running task (unconditionally) to update the task-row state. Extend it to also set the corresponding stage card's status to `\"Flagged\"` when it processes a `flagged` event. This piggybacks on the existing unconditional path without adding a new guard.\n\n*Clean separation \u2014 task-row and stage-board updates live together in the same method.*\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"ApplyStageEventToBoard is only called for the selected task's events, so a 'flagged' event for a non-selected task never updates the stage card status from 'Running' to 'Flagged', leaving the 1-second elapsed timer ticking indefinitely on the review stage card.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"A: In HandleRelayEvent, update the stage board status to 'Flagged' for flagged events unconditionally, before the selected-task guard \u2014 stops the timer regardless of which task is selected.\",\n    \"B: In ResetStages / BeginRunningTask, stop any stage still showing 'Running' when a new task begins, cleaning up orphaned timers defensively.\",\n    \"C: In UpdateRunningTask (which already handles flagged events unconditionally for running tasks), also set the corresponding stage card's status to 'Flagged', piggybacking on the existing unconditional path.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
    "exit_code": 0
